  "preview": "Anchor on 1 ft = 12 in. Vary the count, then the unit pair (yards, gallons, metric). Pairs with Fluency drills.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Students treat each conversion as a ratio (e.g., 12 in : 1 ft) and apply it consistently — even when the source unit is fractional or when converting in the reverse direction.",
    "launch": "Write '1 ft = 12 in' on the board. Don't talk about multiplication factors — just ask: '2 ft = ? in.' The pattern itself teaches the rule.",
    "concepts": [
      "unit-conversion",
      "ratios",
      "measurement"
    ],
    "prerequisiteLabel": "Warm up: what does each conversion mean?",
    "prerequisiteSteps": [
      {
        "problem": "1 ft = ? inches",
        "answer": "12 in"
      },
      {
        "problem": "1 lb = ? ounces",
        "answer": "16 oz"
      },
      {
        "problem": "1 yard = ? feet",
        "answer": "3 ft"
      },
      {
        "problem": "1 gallon = ? quarts",
        "answer": "4 qt"
      }
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "problem": "2 ft = ? in",
        "answer": "24 in",
        "teacherNote": "Anchor — multiply by 12."
      },
      {
        "problem": "5 ft = ? in",
        "answer": "60 in",
        "teacherNote": "Same rule."
      },
      {
        "problem": "1.5 ft = ? in",
        "answer": "18 in",
        "teacherNote": "Fractional input — the multiplier rule still works."
      },
      {
        "problem": "1/2 ft = ? in",
        "answer": "6 in",
        "teacherNote": "Same idea in fraction form."
      },
      {
        "problem": "36 in = ? ft",
        "answer": "3 ft",
        "teacherNote": "Reverse direction — divide by 12 instead of multiply."
      },
      {
        "problem": "30 in = ? ft",
        "answer": "2.5 ft",
        "teacherNote": "Reverse with fractional result."
      },
      {
        "problem": "3 yd = ? ft",
        "answer": "9 ft",
        "teacherNote": "Switch units: 1 yd = 3 ft."
      },
      {
        "problem": "12 ft = ? yd",
        "answer": "4 yd",
        "teacherNote": "Reverse direction, same conversion."
      },
      {
        "problem": "2 lb = ? oz",
        "answer": "32 oz",
        "teacherNote": "Switch unit pair entirely: 1 lb = 16 oz."
      },
      {
        "problem": "48 oz = ? lb",
        "answer": "3 lb",
        "teacherNote": "Reverse."
      },
      {
        "problem": "3 gal = ? qt",
        "answer": "12 qt",
        "teacherNote": "Switch to liquid units."
      },
      {
        "problem": "1 m = ? cm",
        "answer": "100 cm",
        "teacherNote": "Switch to metric: 1 m = 100 cm."
      }
    ],
    "enrichmentSteps": [
      {
        "problem": "2.5 yd = ? in",
        "answer": "90 in",
        "teacherNote": "Chain: yd → ft → in. 2.5 × 3 × 12 = 90."
      },
      {
        "problem": "1 gal = ? cups",
        "answer": "16 cups",
        "teacherNote": "Chain: gal → qt → pt → cups."
      },
      {
        "problem": "5 km = ? m",
        "answer": "5000 m",
        "teacherNote": "Metric — multiply by 1000."
      },
      {
        "problem": "150 cm = ? m",
        "answer": "1.5 m",
        "teacherNote": "Reverse direction."
      }
    ],
    "consolidation": "Bring the class back. Ask: 'How do you decide whether to multiply or divide?' Push for 'if you're going to the smaller unit (in, oz, cm), multiply; bigger unit, divide.' Then: 'Why does the same rule work for fractional inputs like 1.5 ft?' The conversion factor is a CONSTANT ratio. Then point them at Fluency Practice for individual reps.",
